<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brough, East Riding of Yorkshire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brough-on-Humber</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brough is a town on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> estuary in the East Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It stands on the site of the Roman fort and town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petuaria">Petuaria</a>, which an inscription names as a vicus of the Parisi with its own aedile, and which probably served as their civitas capital. The settlement guarded a ferry crossing on the Roman road from Lincoln to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brough-east-riding-of-yorkshire.jpg" alt="Brough, East Riding of Yorkshire" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Harrop · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brough_sunflower_sculpture_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8304322.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
